<p>Was brought here per PM request:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>SCP that is believed to be a humanoid anomaly in the form of a mummy.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What kind of mummy are we talking about here? Traditional Ancient Egyptian embalmment? I ask this because you will need to be specific about this in the clinical setting of an SCP document.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The first being it's sarcophagus which is theorized to give the SCP a regenerative property</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The foundation doesn't know? Regeneration is a big claim to make without proof. Also, what kind of regeneration are we talking about here? I ask because as read my immediate next question is &quot;so why is this guy still a mummy if the coffin is regenerating them&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Should these contents be taken more than 3.4M from the sarcophagus the SCP goes into a rage like state killing the thief and those whom attempt to stop it.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That's&#8230; a bit cliche don't you think?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The anomaly is found to be rather cooperative and expresses willing to communicate with foundation personnel,</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why though? Why is it willing to cooperate with the Foundation? To what end? This should be something you touch on in the article, otherwise it's just a skip what is helpful for reasons.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>All in all, it just seems to be a Great Value version of Able, with some ORIA connections thrown in to give it some extra flair and nothing that really stands out to me from the other thing what kills you humanoids.</p>

