<p>Hello primedstasis! let's take a look :)</p>
<p><strong>SAND</strong></p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><strong>The concept:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Elevator Pitch: A collection of objects with anomalous, antidepressive effects.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Overall this is too vague, as there isn't anything giving context to the skip. What exactly are the effects? what are the objects themselves? whats their purpose? ect. Along with this, there isn't anything immediately interesting about the concept. Currently it just sounds like an antidepressant, what about the concept will me as the reader care?</p>
<p><strong>The narrative:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Central Narrative: Discovered in a lab of an unknown GoI. Consists of an air freshener, a room that can only be accessed by smelling the content of the air freshener, and a lavender plant.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The narrative is also rather vague, and looks closer to additional info on the skip. There's only really a setup here. The middle act, conflict and payoff aren't really shown, which makes the story feel unfinished and hard to get invested in. Overall, I'm unsure on What everything is building up to? what happens? does the unknown GOI play any part in anything? what part does the skip play in everything?</p>
<p>The Foundation doesn't seem to get much of a mention here either, what role do they play in narrative? do they test anything, is there anything of note they learn? are there any characters?- if not I would recommend you include at least 1, as they can help with audience engagement, and offer a way to get invested in the narrative.</p>
<p><strong>Hook:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Hook/Attention-Grabber: There's a fourth object which is only known to high clearance ranks. It's a virus that is presumably the cause of major depressive disorder. Cannot be cured with known means.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This isn't really present in the rest of the concept, what part does this play in the narrative? whats its overall purpose? how will it make me invested?</p>
<p>-As a second note, be careful in how you portray an object causing 'depressive disorder'. The topic in general is rather sensitive and can be hard to pull off, as using it as an effect can come off as insensitive.</p>
<p><strong>Red or green?</strong><br />
Due to my afore mentioned concerns, no-greenlight.</p>
<p>Currently the concept is lacking a fair bit of context, I'm unsure on what all of the objects are, what exactly they do, and what their purpose is. The narrative has the same issues, as there isn't any clear story arc or hook. As a result I can't really get invested in the concept.</p>
<p><strong>Notes</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Additional Notes: I've started writing this SCP, but I would like to know if this idea hasn't been implemented. Also, I'm not a native english speaker, so sorri for may bed england</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I wouldn't recommend drafting until the concept has been given the go ahead, as its very likely to change at least a bit. Which in turn creates extra work.</p>
<p>If you would like me review this (or any concept) further, then please feel free to send me a PM or ask via IRC. Response times generally range from 2-12 hours over PMs. If your looking for additional critiques in general, then I would highly recommend contacting the butterfly squad :)</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Cheers,</p>
<p>-RED</p>

